"Paper page - MaxCode: A Max-Reward Reinforcement Learning Framework for Automated Code Optimization (https://huggingface.co/papers/2601.05475)\nciteturn56search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9 months ago; Crawled: 3 weeks ago; arxiv:2601.05475 ... Testing on the KernelBench (CUDA) and PIE (C++) optimization benchmarks shows that MaxCode improves optimized code performance compared to baselines, achieving 20.3% and 10.1% relative improvements in absolute speedup value and relative speedup ranking, respectively.\n\nPapers\n\narxiv:2601.05475\n\nCopy markdown\n\n# MaxCode: A Max-Reward Reinforcement Learning Framework for Automated Code Optimization\n\nPublished on Jan 9\n\nAuthors:\n\n[Button: Jiefu Ou] ,\n\n[Button: Sapana Chaudhary] ,\n\n[Button: Kaj Bostrom] ,\n\n[Button: Nathaniel Weir] ,\n\n[Button: Shuai Zhang] ,\n\n[Button: Huzefa Rangwala] ,\n\n[Button: George Karypis]\n\n## Abstract\n\nMaxCode employs reinforcement learning and search algorithms to enhance LLM-generated code optimization by incorporating execution feedback and reward modeling.\n\nGenerated by Qwen/Qwen2.5-Coder-32B-Instruct\n\nLarge Language Models (LLMs) demonstrate strong capabilities in general coding tasks but encounter two key challenges when optimizing code: (i) the complexity of writing optimized code (such as performant CUDA kernels and competition-level CPU code) requires expertise in systems, algorithms and specific languages and (ii) requires interpretation of performance metrics like timing and device utilization beyond binary correctness. In this work, we explore inference-time search algorithms that guide the LLM to discover better solutions through iterative refinement based on execution feedback. Our approach, called MaxCode unifies existing search methods under a max-reward reinforcement learning framework, making the observation and action-value functions modular for modification. To enhance the observation space, we integrate a natural language critique model that converts raw execution feedback into diagnostic insights about errors and performance bottlenecks, and the best-discounted reward seen so far. Together, these provide richer input to the code proposal function. To improve exploration during search, we train a generative reward-to-go model using action values from rollouts to rerank potential solutions. Testing on the KernelBench (CUDA) and PIE (C++) optimization benchmarks shows that MaxCode improves optimized code performance compared to baselines, achieving 20.3% and 10.1% relative improvements in absolute speedup value and relative speedup ranking, respectively.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\ndblp: MaxCode: A Max-Reward Reinforcement Learning Framework for Automated Code Optimization. (https://dblp.org/rec/journals/corr/abs-2601-05475.html)\nciteturn56search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8 months ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; # \"MaxCode: A Max-Reward Reinforcement Learning Framework for Automated Code ...\" ... DOI: 10.48550/ARXIV.2601.05475\n\n# \"MaxCode: A Max-Reward Reinforcement Learning Framework for Automated Code ...\"\n\n  * help us\n\n    * How can I correct errors in dblp?\n    * contact dblp\n\nJiefu Ou et al. (2026)\n\n  *   * Image: use mirror server\n\nDagstuhl\n\n  * Trier\n\n  * > Home\n\n## Details and statistics\n\n  * show external API response\n\n    * JSON @ openalex.org\n\nsee also:\n\n    * API doc @ openalex.org\n\nDOI: 10.48550/ARXIV.2601.05475\n\naccess: openImage\n\ntype: Informal or Other Publication\n\nmetadata version: 2026-02-11\n\n  * Image: Informal and Other Publications\n\n    * view\n\n      * electronic edition via DOI (open access)\n\n\ndblp key:\n\n      * journals/corr/abs-2601-05475\n\n    * ask others\n\n      * Google\n      * Google Scholar\n      * Semantic Scholar\n      * Internet Archive Scholar\n      * CiteSeerX\n      * PubPeer\n\n    * share record\n\n      * Bluesky\n      * Reddit\n      * BibSonomy\n      * LinkedIn\n\npersistent URL:\n\n      * https://dblp.org/rec/journals/corr/abs-2601-05475\n\nJiefu Ou, Sapana Chaudhary, Kaj Bostrom, Nathaniel Weir, Shuai Zhang, Huzefa Rangwala, George Karypis:\nMaxCode: A Max-Reward Reinforcement Learning Framework for Automated Code Optimization. CoRR abs/2601.05475 (2026)\n\n\nlast updated on 2026-02-11 16:57 CET by the dblp team--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\ndblp: MaxCode: A Max-Reward Reinforcement Learning Framework for Automated Code Optimization. (https://dblp.dagstuhl.de/rec/journals/corr/abs-2601-05475.html)\nciteturn56search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8 months ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; # \"MaxCode: A Max-Reward Reinforcement Learning Framework for Automated Code ...\" ... DOI: 10.48550/ARXIV.2601.05475\n\n# \"MaxCode: A Max-Reward Reinforcement Learning Framework for Automated Code ...\"\n\n  * help us\n\n    * How can I correct errors in dblp?\n    * contact dblp\n\nJiefu Ou et al. (2026)\n\n\n## Details and statistics\n\n  * show external API response\n\n    * JSON @ openalex.org\n\nsee also:\n\n    * API doc @ openalex.org\n\nDOI: 10.48550/ARXIV.2601.05475\n\naccess: openImage\n\ntype: Informal or Other Publication\n\nmetadata version: 2026-02-11\n\n  * Image: Informal and Other Publications\n\n    * view\n\n\ndblp key:\n\n      * journals/corr/abs-2601-05475\n\n    * ask others\n\n      * Google\n      * Google Scholar\n      * Semantic Scholar\n      * Internet Archive Scholar\n      * CiteSeerX\n      * PubPeer\n\n    * share record\n\n      * Bluesky\n      * Reddit\n      * BibSonomy\n      * LinkedIn\n\npersistent URL:\n\n      * https://dblp.org/rec/journals/corr/abs-2601-05475\n\nJiefu Ou, Sapana Chaudhary, Kaj Bostrom, Nathaniel Weir, Shuai Zhang, Huzefa Rangwala, George Karypis:\nMaxCode: A Max-Reward Reinforcement Learning Framework for Automated Code Optimization. CoRR abs/2601.05475 (2026)\n\n\nlast updated on 2026-02-11 16:57 CET by the dblp team\n\n all metadata released as open data under CC0 1.0 license--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMaxCode: A Max-Reward Reinforcement Learning Framework for Automated Code Optimization | alphaXiv (https://www.alphaxiv.org/abs/2601.05475)\nciteturn56search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9 months ago; Crawled: last month; @misc{ou2026maxcodemaxrewardreinforcementlearning, title={MaxCode: A Max-Reward Reinforcement Learning Framework for Automated Code Optimization}, author={Jiefu Ou and Sapana Chaudhary and Kaj Bostrom and Nathaniel Weir and Shuai Zhang and Huzefa Rangwala and George Karypis}, year={2026}, eprint={2601.05475}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={cs.LG}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.05475}, }\n## Generative Value Function for Efficient Search\n\nTo address the computational expense of evaluating candidate solutions, MAXCODE introduces a learned generative value function approximator Ṽφ(st, ut). This component aims to predict the potential future performance of candidate code revisions before costly full execution.\n\nThe training process involves:\n\n  1. Data Collection: Sampling search trajectories and computing target values v*t as the maximum of ut/γ and the best discounted future reward from state st\n  2. Categorical Formulation: Discretizing continuous speedup values into five categorical bins (0-4) to manage prediction variance\n  3. Model Training: Training a smaller LLM (Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct) to predict categorical distributions over reward categories\n\nDuring guided search, this learned value function estimates expected future maximum performance for candidate states, enabling intelligent prioritization of which candidates to expand based on predicted potential rather than just immediate rewards.\n\n## Experimental Results and Performance\n\nThe evaluation on KernelBench (CUDA kernel optimization) and PIE (competitive C++ optimization) benchmarks demonstrates significant improvements across multiple metrics. When integrated with CUDA-LLM, MAXCODE achieved relative speedup improvements of 27.3% on KernelBench Level 1, 11.0% on Level 2, and 22.5% on PIE.\n\nImage: Performance Results Figure: Aggregated results showing MaxCode's performance improvements across different optimization tasks and metrics.\n\nAblation studies revealed that the combination of trajectory information with natural language critiques yielded the highest performance on most benchmarks, while adding explicit \"best performance\" tracking provided additional benefits for more complex optimization tasks. The framework also demonstrated superior inference-time scaling, achieving higher median maximum speedups more quickly than baseline methods for the same computational budget.\n\n## Unique Contributions and Significance\n\nMAXCODE makes several distinctive contributions that differentiate it from existing approaches:\n\n  1. Principled Formalization: The max-reward RL formulation provides a theoretically grounded framework that naturally aligns with code optimization objectives, unlike cumulative reward approaches that may lead to suboptimal solutions.\n\n  2. Actionable Feedback Translation: The natural language critique model bridges the gap between raw numerical metrics and the kind of diagnostic reasoning LLMs need for effective optimization, moving beyond simple error messages to provide interpretable insights.\n\n  3. Explicit Best-Performance Tracking: By incorporating the best reward achieved so far directly into the state representation, the framework ensures LLMs maintain awareness of their optimization targets throughout the search process.\n\n  4. Framework Generalizability: The approach enhances existing search methods rather than replacing them, making it broadly applicable to various code optimization scenarios and base algorithms.\n\nThe learned value function component, while showing mixed results, represents an important step toward more efficient search guidance. The challenges encountered highlight both the potential and current limitations of using predictive models for fine-grained optimization guidance, opening directions for future research in this area.\n\nThis work represents a significant advancement in automated code optimization, providing LLMs with both better understanding of performance feedback and a more targeted optimization objective. The framework's ability to improve existing methods while maintaining broad applicability makes it a valuable contribution to the growing field of AI-assisted software engineering.\n\nTo the max: Reinventing reward in reinforcement learning\n\nThis paper provides the core theoretical foundation for the max-reward reinforcement learning formulation, which is the central concept that the MaxCode framework is built upon and named after.\n\nGrigorii Veviurko, Wendelin B¨ohmer, and Mathijs De Weerdt. To the max: Reinventing reward in reinforcement learning. arXiv preprint arXiv:2402.01361, 2024.\n\nKernelbench: Can llms write efficient gpu kernels?\n\nThis paper introduces KernelBench, one of the two primary benchmarks used to evaluate MaxCode's performance. The ability to optimize CUDA kernels on this benchmark is a key demonstration of MaxCode's effectiveness.\n\nAnne Ouyang, Simon Guo, Simran Arora, Alex L. Zhang, William Hu, Christopher R’e, and Azalia Mirhoseini. Kernelbench: Can llms write efficient gpu kernels? ArXiv, abs/2502.10517, 2025. URL https://api.semanticscholar.org/CorpusID:276408165.\n\nLearning performance-improving code edits\n\nThis work introduced the PIE benchmark for optimizing competitive C++ code. It is the second key benchmark used in the paper to demonstrate the generalizability of the MaxCode framework beyond GPU kernel optimization.\n\nAman Madaan, Alex Shypula, Uri Alon, Milad Hashemi, Parthasarathy Ranganathan, Yiming Yang, Graham Neubig, and Amir Yazdanbakhsh. Learning performance-improving code edits. ArXiv, abs/2302.07867, 2023. URL https://api.semanticscholar.org/CorpusID:256868633.\n\nCuda-llm: Llms can write efficient cuda kernels\n\nThis paper proposes a beam-search based method that is a key baseline in the MaxCode paper. MaxCode reformulates this method within its max-reward framework and shows significant performance gains, highlighting its ability to enhance existing search techniques.\n\nWentao Chen, Jiace Zhu, Qi Fan, Yehan Ma, and An Zou. Cuda-llm: Llms can write efficient cuda kernels. ArXiv, abs/2506.09092, 2025. URL https://api.semanticscholar.org/CorpusID:279305780.\n\nTeaching language models to critique via reinforcement learning\n\nThis work is cited as the inspiration for a core component of MaxCode: the use of a natural language critique model to enrich the observation space. This component converts raw execution feedback into diagnostic insights, which is a key contribution of the main paper.\n\nZhihui Xie, Jie chen, Liyu Chen, Weichao Mao, Jingjing Xu, and Lingpeng Kong. Teaching language models to critique via reinforcement learning. ArXiv, abs/2502.03492, 2025. URL https://api.semanticscholar.org/CorpusID:276161646.\n\n## Citation\n\nCopy\n\n@misc{ou2026maxcodemaxrewardreinforcementlearning, title={MaxCode: A Max-Reward Reinforcement Learning Framework for Automated Code Optimization}, author={Jiefu Ou and Sapana Chaudhary and Kaj Bostrom and Nathaniel Weir and Shuai Zhang and Huzefa Rangwala and George Karypis}, year={2026}, eprint={2601.05475}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={cs.LG}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.05475}, }--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\npaper-notes/docs/automated-kernel-generation/maxcode.md at main · zksainx/paper-notes · GitHub (https://github.com/zksainx/paper-notes/blob/main/docs/automated-kernel-generation/maxcode.md)\nciteturn56search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; Paper Link: arXiv:2601.05475 ...   * MaxCode extends iterative code optimization with a state that includes execution feedback, natural-language critique, and the best reward seen so far.\n\n# MaxCode: A Max-Reward Reinforcement Learning Framework for Automated Code Optimization\n\nAuthors: Huzefa Rangwala, George Karypis\nInstitution: Johns Hopkins University, Amazon Web Services\nConference: arXiv\nYear: 2026\nPaper Link: arXiv:2601.05475\n\nCode Optimization Max-Reward RL Inference-Time Search\n\n## Background\n\nMaxCode studies optimization tasks where a language model starts from correct but slow code and must iteratively produce faster variants. This is harder than ordinary code generation because success depends on both functional correctness and hardware-sensitive performance. Raw execution feedback such as compile errors, test failures, or a single speedup number is often too weak to explain why a candidate is slow or what transformation should come next.\n\nThe key reframing is to treat code optimization as a max-reward reinforcement learning problem. The objective is not cumulative reward across all intermediate steps, but the best performance found anywhere along the search trajectory. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\narXivDaily每日学术速递，同步arXiv全量数据，AI总结、翻译，覆盖人工智能、机器人、计算机、金融、统计学、数学、物理学、生物学、经济学、电气&系统等方向。 (https://www.arxivdaily.com/?date=2026-01-12&major=CS&search_in=all&subcat=cs.CL)\nciteturn56search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9 months ago; Crawled: 4 months ago; 2601.05475 2026-01-12 cs.LG cs.CL 版本更新 ... Testing on the KernelBench (CUDA) and PIE (C++) optimization benchmarks shows that MaxCode improves optimized code performance compared to baselines, achieving 20.3% and 10.1% relative improvements in absolute speedup value and relative speedup ranking, respectively.\nTo address this, we propose Deceptive Intent Shielding (DIS), a governance mechanism that provides an early warning signal by inferring the deceptive intent behind evidence. Empirical results demonstrate that DIS consistently mitigates belief shifts and promotes more cautious evidence evaluation.\n\nURL PDF HTML ☆ [Button: 复制]\n\n赞 0 踩 0\n\n2601.05475 2026-01-12 cs.LG cs.CL 版本更新\n\n## MaxCode: A Max-Reward Reinforcement Learning Framework for Automated Code Optimization\n\nJiefu Ou, Sapana Chaudhary, Kaj Bostrom, Nathaniel Weir, Shuai Zhang, Huzefa Rangwala, George Karypis\n\n详情\n\n英文摘要\n\nLarge Language Models (LLMs) demonstrate strong capabilities in general coding tasks but encounter two key challenges when optimizing code: (i) the complexity of writing optimized code (such as performant CUDA kernels and competition-level CPU code) requires expertise in systems, algorithms and specific languages and (ii) requires interpretation of performance metrics like timing and device utilization beyond binary correctness. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSearch | arXiv e-print repository (https://rt.http3.lol/index.php?q=aHR0cHM6Ly9hcnhpdi5vcmcvc2VhcmNoL2NzP3NlYXJjaHR5cGU9YXV0aG9yJnF1ZXJ5PU91LCtK)\nciteturn56search6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday;   35. arXiv:2601.05475  [pdf, ps, other] ... Testing on the KernelBench (CUDA) and PIE (C++) optimization benchmarks shows that MaxCode improves optimized code performance compared to baselines, achieving 20.3% and 10.1% relative improvements in absolute speedup value and relative speedup ranking, respectively.\nSubmitted 14 January, 2026; originally announced January 2026.\n\nComments: Accepted by EACL 2026\n\n  35. arXiv:2601.05475  [pdf, ps, other] \n\ncs.LG cs.CL\n\nMaxCode: A Max-Reward Reinforcement Learning Framework for Automated Code Optimization\n\nAuthors: Jiefu Ou, Sapana Chaudhary, Kaj Bostrom, Nathaniel Weir, Shuai Zhang, Huzefa Rangwala, George Karypis\n\nAbstract: Large Language Models (LLMs) demonstrate strong capabilities in general coding tasks but encounter two key challenges when optimizing code: (i) the complexity of writing optimized code (such as performant CUDA kernels and competition-level CPU code) requires expertise in systems, alg